A font file is the resource most likely to be both **render-blocking in effect** and **discovered late**. It is also the one with a purely declarative fix.

![Web font loading — FOIT, FOUT and the fix](/diagrams/webfund/font-loading.svg)

## Why fonts arrive late

The chain is: HTML → stylesheet → parse CSS → match `@font-face` → *only now* request the font. Two round trips before the request starts, and the browser will not even ask for a font that no element on the page currently uses.

Meanwhile the text has to be drawn in something. The two failure modes have names:

- **FOIT** — Flash of Invisible Text. The browser hides the text while it waits. Historically up to 3 seconds of nothing where your headline should be.
- **FOUT** — Flash of Unstyled Text. It draws the fallback immediately and swaps when the web font arrives. Text is readable at once, but it moves — a layout shift, which is a [Core Web Vitals](/system-design/402) problem.

## `font-display` picks your poison

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Inter';
  src: url('/fonts/inter-var.woff2') format('woff2-variations');
  font-weight: 100 900;
  font-display: swap;
  /* Match the fallback's metrics so the swap does not move the text. */
  size-adjust: 107%;
  ascent-override: 90%;
}
```

| Value | Block period | Swap period | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `auto` | Browser default (usually ~3 s) | — | FOIT |
| `block` | ~3 s | Infinite | FOIT, deliberately |
| `swap` | 0 | Infinite | FOUT — text is instantly readable |
| `fallback` | ~100 ms | ~3 s | Brief FOIT, then give up permanently |
| `optional` | ~100 ms | 0 | Use it if it is already cached, otherwise never — **zero shift** |

`swap` for body text: readable immediately beats pretty eventually. `optional` for anything decorative, and for a repeat-visit-heavy site where the font will be in cache anyway.

## Removing the shift, not just the invisibility

`swap` trades invisible text for moving text. The fix is to make the fallback occupy the same space as the real font:

```css
/* A fallback face that is metrically matched to the web font. */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Inter fallback';
  src: local('Arial');
  size-adjust: 107%;
  ascent-override: 90%;
  descent-override: 22%;
  line-gap-override: 0%;
}
body { font-family: 'Inter', 'Inter fallback', sans-serif; }
```

Now the swap changes the glyph shapes and nothing else moves. This is the single highest-value font technique and almost nobody does it.

## Cut the bytes

- **`woff2` only.** Every browser you support has it; shipping `woff`, `ttf` and `eot` alongside is dead weight in the `src` list.
- **Subset.** A Latin subset of a typical face is 15–25 kB; the full multi-script file is 200 kB+. `unicode-range` lets you ship several subsets and have the browser download only the ones the page actually uses.
- **A variable font replaces a family.** One 40 kB variable file instead of regular + medium + bold + their italics is usually a large net win — but only if you use three or more weights.
- **Self-host.** A third-party font host means an extra DNS + TCP + [TLS handshake](/browser/160), no cache sharing across sites (partitioned caches killed that), and a third party in your critical path. Copy the file into your own origin behind your own [CDN](/system-design/115).

```html
<!-- Self-hosted and preloaded: the request starts with the HTML, not two round trips later. -->
<link
  rel="preload"
  as="font"
  type="font/woff2"
  href="/fonts/inter-var.woff2"
  crossorigin
/>
```

`crossorigin` is required even for a same-origin font, because fonts are always fetched in [CORS](/browser/156) mode — without it you get two downloads. See [resource hints](/web-fundamentals/3).

## Key takeaways

- Fonts are discovered two round trips late; `preload` them, with `crossorigin`.
- `font-display: swap` for text you need read, `optional` for decoration.
- Metric-matched fallbacks (`size-adjust`, `ascent-override`) remove the layout shift `swap` causes.
- `woff2`, subset, self-hosted. Variable fonts pay off from about three weights.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"FOIT or FOUT?"** — FOUT, with a metric-matched fallback so the swap does not shift anything. Invisible text is a worse experience than the wrong shapes.
- **"Why did preloading the font make no difference?"** — Usually a `crossorigin` mismatch, so the preload was not reused and the font downloaded twice.
- **"What is `font-display: optional` for?"** — Zero layout shift: the font is used only if it is already cached, so a first visit renders in the fallback and later visits get the real thing.

Two module systems that look similar and behave differently, and every [bundler](/web-fundamentals/9) config problem you will ever hit lives in the gap between them.

## The difference that causes everything else

**ESM is static. CJS is dynamic.**

```javascript
// ESM — the imports are part of the syntax. Resolvable without running the file.
import { debounce } from "./utils.js";
export const wait = 300;

// CJS — `require` is a function call. You only know what it loads by executing it.
const { debounce } = require("./utils");
if (process.env.NODE_ENV === "test") {
  module.exports = require("./mock"); // legal, and unanalysable
}
```

Because ESM's dependency graph is known before execution, a bundler can build the graph, follow which exports are used, and drop the rest. That is [tree shaking](/web-fundamentals/10), and it is why the static/dynamic distinction matters commercially rather than academically.

## The behaviour differences worth knowing

| | ESM | CommonJS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Resolution | Static, at parse time | Dynamic, at call time |
| Loading | Asynchronous | Synchronous |
| Bindings | **Live** — the importer sees later reassignments | A **copy** of the value at require time |
| `this` at top level | `undefined` | `module.exports` |
| [Strict mode](/learnings/102) | Always | Only if declared |
| [Hoisting](/learnings/99) | Imports are hoisted, and evaluated before the body | `require` runs where it is written |
| Circular deps | Handled via the binding graph; may see a TDZ error | Returns a partially-populated `exports` object |
| Available in browsers | Yes, natively | No, needs a bundler |

Live bindings are the surprise:

```javascript
// counter.js
export let count = 0;
export const inc = () => {
  count += 1;
};

// main.js
import { count, inc } from "./counter.js";
inc();
console.log(count); // 1 — the binding, not a snapshot
```

The CJS equivalent logs `0`, because the importer took a copy.

## Dynamic import is the bridge

```javascript
// Returns a promise, works in both worlds, and is a code-split point.
const { Chart } = await import("./chart.js");
```

It is the only way to load a module conditionally in ESM, and it is how route-level splitting is expressed — the bundler sees the `import()` and emits a separate chunk.

## What actually happens in your app

- **Your source is ESM.** You write `import`/`export` and the bundler resolves the graph.
- **Some dependency is CJS.** The bundler wraps it in an interop shim, and that shim usually cannot be tree-shaken — a CJS dependency drags in its whole file.
- **The published package tells the bundler which to use**:

```json
{
  "name": "my-lib",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/index.mjs",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    }
  },
  "sideEffects": false
}
```

`exports` is the modern field and it is exclusive: once it exists, anything not listed cannot be imported from the package, deep import paths included. `sideEffects: false` is the promise that tree shaking depends on.

## Native modules in the browser

```html
<script type="module" src="/app.mjs"></script>
```

Deferred by default, always strict, fetched with [CORS](/browser/156), and each module executes **once** no matter how many files import it. Native ESM is real, but a bundler still earns its place: it resolves bare specifiers (`import React from "react"` is not a URL), and it stops a deep import graph from becoming hundreds of waterfalled requests.

## Key takeaways

- ESM is statically analysable; CJS is not, and that single fact drives tree shaking, interop pain and bundle size.
- ESM has live bindings, async loading and always-strict semantics.
- `import()` is the dynamic escape hatch and the code-split boundary.
- `exports` and `sideEffects` in `package.json` are what let a bundler do its job.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"Why can't CommonJS be tree-shaken?"** — Its exports are computed at runtime, so a tool cannot prove which ones go unused without executing the module.
- **"What is a live binding?"** — The importer references the exporter's variable, not a copy, so a later reassignment inside the module is visible to everyone who imported it.
- **"Why do you still need a bundler if browsers support modules?"** — Bare specifier resolution, dependency graphs of hundreds of files, minification, and the chunking strategy.

`fetch` is three things people get wrong in the same interview: it does not reject on 404, its body is a stream you can only read once, and it has no timeout.

## It only rejects on a network failure

```javascript
const res = await fetch("/api/user/42");
// res.ok is false for 404 and 500 — but nothing threw.
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const user = await res.json();
```

A rejected `fetch` means the request never completed: DNS failure, connection refused, [CORS](/browser/156) blocked it, or it was aborted. Every HTTP status — including 500 — is a successful fetch. Wrapping `fetch` in a helper that checks `res.ok` is the first thing any real codebase does.

Note that a CORS failure is deliberately opaque: you get a `TypeError` with no detail, and the reason is only in the console.

## The body is a stream, and you get one read

```javascript
const res = await fetch("/api/report");
const text = await res.text();
const json = await res.json(); // ✗ TypeError: body stream already read
```

`res.clone()` before the first read if you genuinely need two. That single-read rule is the same reason a `Response` from the [Cache API](/web-fundamentals/15) has to be cloned before you both return and store it.

Reading it as a stream is what makes token-by-token UI possible:

```javascript
const res = await fetch("/api/chat", { method: "POST", body });
const reader = res.body.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream()).getReader();

for (;;) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  append(value); // partial text, as it arrives
}
```

That is the mechanism behind [streaming AI responses](/ai-dev/6) and progressive server rendering.

## Cancellation

```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();
const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
controller.abort(); // → the fetch rejects with an AbortError
```

`AbortSignal` is not fetch-specific: `addEventListener(…, { signal })` removes the listener on abort, and your own async helpers should accept a signal and check `signal.aborted`. One controller can cancel a whole tree of work.

There is no `timeout` option, so build one:

```javascript
// AbortSignal.timeout(5000) does this natively where available.
async function request(url, { timeout = 5000, signal } = {}) {
  const merged = AbortSignal.any(
    [AbortSignal.timeout(timeout), signal].filter(Boolean),
  );
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: merged });
  if (!res.ok)
    throw Object.assign(new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`), {
      status: res.status,
    });
  return res.json();
}
```

Two patterns worth naming out loud in an interview:

- **Abort the previous request** on every new keystroke, so a slow earlier response cannot overwrite a newer one. Abort alone is not sufficient — a request already in flight can still land, so keep a sequence number too.
- **Abort on unmount**, so a resolved promise does not set state on a component that is gone.

## The options that catch people

| Option | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| `credentials: 'include'` | Cookies are **not** sent cross-origin without it — see [cookies and storage](/browser/132) |
| `mode: 'cors'` | The default for cross-origin; `no-cors` gives you an opaque response you cannot read |
| `cache: 'no-store'` | Bypass the HTTP cache for a version check or a poll |
| `keepalive: true` | Lets a small request outlive the page — the right way to send a final beacon |
| `redirect: 'manual'` | When you need to see the 3xx rather than follow it |

## Retries, briefly

Retry only idempotent requests, only on a network error or a 5xx, with [exponential backoff](/system-design/410) and jitter, and give up after two or three attempts. Retrying a `POST /orders` is how a customer gets charged twice; an idempotency key is what makes it safe.

## Key takeaways

- `fetch` rejects only on network failure — check `res.ok` yourself, always.
- The body is a single-use stream: `clone()` to read twice, or read it as a stream for progressive UI.
- No timeout is built in; compose `AbortSignal.timeout` with your own signal.
- Cookies need `credentials: 'include'` cross-origin.
- Retry idempotent requests only, with backoff.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"Does `fetch` throw on a 500?"** — No. Only a network-level failure rejects; a 500 is a fulfilled promise with `ok: false`.
- **"How do you cancel a search-as-you-type request?"** — An `AbortController` per keystroke, aborting the previous — plus a sequence guard, because an aborted request may already have landed.
- **"Why is the response empty after you logged it?"** — The body was already consumed; `console.log(res)` then `res.json()` is fine, but two reads are not.

A service worker is a **proxy you write in JavaScript**, running in its own thread, sitting between your page and the network. That single sentence explains both the power and every footgun.

![The service worker — interception and the two cache strategies](/diagrams/webfund/service-worker.svg)

## The lifecycle

```javascript
// In the page.
navigator.serviceWorker.register("/sw.js"); // scope defaults to /
```

1. **Install** — first registration, or whenever the bytes of `sw.js` change. Precache the shell here.
2. **Waiting** — the new worker sits idle while the old one still controls open pages. This is the step people trip over.
3. **Activate** — takes control once the old one is gone; clean up stale caches here.
4. **Fetch** — every request in scope now passes through it, until it is replaced.

```javascript
const CACHE = "shell-v4";

self.addEventListener("install", (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches
      .open(CACHE)
      .then((c) => c.addAll(["/", "/offline.html", "/assets/app.css"])),
  );
});

self.addEventListener("activate", (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches
      .keys()
      .then((keys) =>
        Promise.all(
          keys.filter((k) => k !== CACHE).map((k) => caches.delete(k)),
        ),
      ),
  );
});
```

**A page loaded before the worker existed is not controlled by it.** Registration takes effect on the next navigation unless you call `clients.claim()` in `activate` — which is fine for a first install and dangerous mid-session, because the page then starts talking to a worker whose assumptions it was not built for.

## The interception point

```javascript
self.addEventListener("fetch", (event) => {
  const { request } = event;
  if (request.method !== "GET") return; // never cache a mutation

  // Hashed assets: cache-first. The name changes when the content does.
  if (request.url.includes("/assets/")) {
    event.respondWith(
      caches.match(request).then(
        (hit) =>
          hit ??
          fetch(request).then((res) => {
            const copy = res.clone(); // the body is single-use
            caches.open(CACHE).then((c) => c.put(request, copy));
            return res;
          }),
      ),
    );
    return;
  }

  // Navigations: network-first, fall back to the offline page.
  if (request.mode === "navigate") {
    event.respondWith(
      fetch(request).catch(() => caches.match("/offline.html")),
    );
  }
});
```

| Strategy | Use for | Risk |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Cache-first** | Hashed assets, fonts, images | Serves stale forever if the URL is not versioned |
| **Network-first** | HTML, API GETs | Slow when the network is slow-but-alive |
| **Stale-while-revalidate** | Avatars, feeds, anything a little stale is fine | The user sees the previous value once |
| **Network-only** | Anything authenticated or mutating | — |

## Step through one request

```trace
{
  "input": "Three navigations through a service worker with a precached shell",
  "lanes": [
    { "id": "req", "label": "Request", "kind": "slots", "slots": ["url", "network"] },
    { "id": "sw", "label": "Service worker decision", "kind": "phases", "of": ["match cache", "hit", "go to network", "store", "respond"] },
    { "id": "log", "label": "What the user gets", "kind": "log" }
  ],
  "vars": ["strategy", "served from"],
  "steps": [
    {
      "note": "A hashed asset, already precached. The worker answers without touching the network at all.",
      "cells": { "req": ["/assets/app.8f3a.js", "online"], "log": ["cache hit — 3 ms"] },
      "marks": { "sw": { "match cache": "active", "hit": "hit", "respond": "done" } },
      "vars": { "strategy": "cache-first", "served from": "Cache Storage" }
    },
    {
      "note": "A new hashed chunk after a deploy. Miss, so the worker fetches it, clones the response, stores the copy and returns the original.",
      "cells": { "req": ["/assets/feed.2d77.js", "online"], "log": ["cache hit — 3 ms", "miss → network 180 ms → stored"] },
      "marks": { "sw": { "match cache": "active", "go to network": "active", "store": "window", "respond": "done" } },
      "vars": { "strategy": "cache-first", "served from": "network, then cached" }
    },
    {
      "note": "A navigation while offline. Network-first throws, and the `catch` serves the precached offline page instead of the browser's error screen.",
      "cells": { "req": ["/dashboard", "offline"], "log": ["miss → network 180 ms → stored", "network failed → /offline.html"] },
      "marks": { "sw": { "go to network": "bad", "match cache": "active", "respond": "hit" } },
      "vars": { "strategy": "network-first", "served from": "Cache Storage (fallback)" }
    }
  ]
}
```

## The app shell model

The architecture the precaching above is *for*. Split the page in two:

| Half | What it is | Where it comes from |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **The shell** | the minimum HTML, CSS and JS to draw the frame — nav, header, sidebar, layout, base styles | precached at install; served from Cache Storage, instantly |
| **The content** | the actual data, usually JSON | fetched per view, or read from [IndexedDB](/web-fundamentals/16) when offline |

```javascript
// install: the shell is static and versioned, so cache-first is safe.
const SHELL = ["/", "/assets/app.css", "/assets/app.js", "/offline.html"];

self.addEventListener("install", (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(caches.open(CACHE).then((c) => c.addAll(SHELL)));
});
```

**First visit** loads over the network and the worker precaches the shell.
**Every visit after** serves the shell from local cache before a packet leaves the
machine, then hydrates it with fresh data. The user sees a real interface at
roughly disk-read speed rather than at network speed, which is most of why a PWA
feels native.

What it buys you, concretely: FCP stops depending on the network, and the layout
is present before the data is, so there is no blank screen and no layout shift
when the content lands — you are rendering into a frame that already exists.

The catch is that the shell is now **versioned state on the user's device**. Ship
a new shell and every returning user runs the old one until the next activation,
so the shell and the API it calls have to stay compatible across at least one
version — the same [stale-tab problem](/system-design/109) as any deploy, except
the stale copy can be days old.

Two rules keep it honest:

- **Nothing user-specific in the shell.** It is shared by every visitor on the
  device and served before auth is checked; personalised markup baked into it
  leaks between accounts.
- **Never cache-first the data.** The shell is immutable and versioned, the data
  is not. Cache-first for the shell, network-first (or stale-while-revalidate)
  for anything from an API.

## The traps

- **Caching the HTML with cache-first.** Now your deploy never reaches anyone, and the fix is behind the cached HTML too — the [same outage as caching HTML at the CDN](/system-design/115), except this copy lives on the user's device and you cannot purge it.
- **`skipWaiting()` without a prompt.** The new worker takes over mid-session and the open page starts getting responses from a version it was not built for. Prompt, then reload.
- **Not versioning the cache name**, so `activate` never cleans up and storage grows until the browser evicts it.
- **Caching authenticated responses.** They land in a shared cache on a shared device.
- **Registering `sw.js` from a subdirectory**, which silently limits scope to that path.
- **No dev story.** "Update on reload" and "Bypass for network" in DevTools exist because a stale worker will otherwise waste your afternoon.

## What else it unlocks

Background sync (queue a failed POST until connectivity returns), push notifications, and periodic sync. All of it is the same trade: a long-lived script with its own lifecycle that your page does not control.

For the wider offline design — conflict resolution, queued mutations, the data model — see [Offline-first and background sync](/system-design/106).

## Key takeaways

- A service worker is a proxy you write; it intercepts every in-scope request.
- Install → waiting → activate → fetch. The waiting step is where the update bugs live.
- Cache-first for hashed assets, network-first for HTML, never for mutations.
- `respondWith` a clone: a `Response` body can only be read once.
- Version the cache name and clean up in `activate`.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"How does a user get the new version?"** — The new worker installs and waits; on the next navigation with no other tabs it activates. If you want it sooner, prompt and then `skipWaiting()` plus reload — never silently.
- **"What would you never cache?"** — Mutations, authenticated responses, and the HTML with a cache-first strategy.
- **"Offline: what does the user see?"** — A precached shell and offline page, cached GET data marked as stale, and queued writes that replay on reconnect.

Six places, and the choice is nearly always decided by three questions: does the **server** need it, does it survive a **reload**, and is it **secret**?

## The map

| Store | Size | Lifetime | Sync? | Server sees it | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Memory** (a variable, a store) | RAM | Until reload | — | No | Anything derived, and access tokens |
| **Cookie** | ~4 KB | You set it | Sync | **Yes, every request** | Session id, server-rendered preferences |
| **`localStorage`** | ~5–10 MB | Forever | **Sync — blocks the main thread** | No | Theme, drafts, small caches |
| **`sessionStorage`** | ~5 MB | The tab | Sync | No | Wizard progress, per-tab filters |
| **IndexedDB** | Large (quota-based) | Forever | Async | No | Structured data, offline records, blobs |
| **Cache Storage** | Large (quota-based) | Forever | Async | No | `Response` objects, via a [service worker](/web-fundamentals/15) |

Scoping, expiry and the cookie attributes are their own topic: [Cookies, localStorage, and sessionStorage](/browser/132).

## Choosing, in three questions

1. **Does the server need it on every request?** → Cookie. Nothing else is sent automatically.
2. **Must it survive a reload?** → No: memory. Yes, and it is small and simple: `localStorage`. Yes, and it is large or structured: IndexedDB.
3. **Is it sensitive?** → Not `localStorage`, ever. An `HttpOnly` cookie, or memory with a refresh cookie behind it.

## `localStorage` is synchronous, and that is the catch

Every read and write blocks the main thread. A 2 MB JSON blob parsed on boot is a long task before your first paint — the kind of thing that shows up as a bad [INP or LCP](/system-design/402).

```javascript
// Also: it throws. Private mode, blocked site data, and quota all reject.
function readJSON(key, fallback) {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
    return raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : fallback;
  } catch {
    return fallback; // never let storage break the render
  }
}
```

It is strings only, so everything round-trips through `JSON.stringify` — and `undefined` becomes the string `"undefined"`, which then fails to parse. Wrap it once and never call it raw.

## IndexedDB, honestly

The native API is event-based and unpleasant; use a thin wrapper (`idb` is ~1 kB) rather than writing the raw transaction plumbing. It earns its place when you have real records: an offline inbox, a cached dataset, a queue of pending writes, files and blobs.

```javascript
import { openDB } from "idb";

const db = await openDB("app", 1, {
  upgrade(db) {
    const store = db.createObjectStore("messages", { keyPath: "id" });
    store.createIndex("by-thread", "threadId");
  },
});

await db.put("messages", {
  id: "m1",
  threadId: "t1",
  body: "…",
  pending: true,
});
const pending = await db.getAllFromIndex("messages", "by-thread", "t1");
```

## Two things people forget

**Storage is evictable.** Under pressure the browser clears origin data for sites the user rarely visits, and Safari clears script-writable storage after seven days of no interaction. `navigator.storage.persist()` asks for an exemption; the answer may be no. **Never treat browser storage as the source of truth** — it is a cache of something the server owns.

**Tabs share it.** `localStorage` writes fire a `storage` event in *other* tabs, which is the cheap way to sync a logout across them. For anything richer, use `BroadcastChannel`:

```javascript
const bus = new BroadcastChannel("auth");
bus.postMessage({ type: "logout" }); // every other tab hears this
bus.onmessage = (e) => e.data.type === "logout" && redirectToLogin();
```

## Key takeaways

- Server needs it → cookie. Survives reload → `localStorage` (small) or IndexedDB (large). Secret → not `localStorage`.
- `localStorage` is synchronous, string-only, and throws; wrap every access.
- IndexedDB for structured or large data, with a small wrapper.
- Everything except cookies is per-origin, evictable, and not a source of truth.
- `storage` events and `BroadcastChannel` keep tabs in step.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"Where would you keep an auth token?"** — An `HttpOnly` cookie, or in memory with a refresh cookie behind it. Never `localStorage`, because any [XSS](/browser/158) reads it.
- **"Why is your app slow to boot?"** — Often a large synchronous `localStorage` read and parse before the first paint.
- **"How do you log the user out of every tab?"** — `BroadcastChannel`, or the `storage` event as the zero-dependency version.
